    "chunk_size_bytes": 1969,
    "chunk_text": "Dr Andrew Racine, president of the American Academy of Pediatrics, said Trump's executive order was \"dangerous\" and not based on \"gold-standard science\".\n\n\"Delaying or skipping shots is risky, especially as measles continues to spread and children go back to school,\" Racine said.\n\n\"The President does not have the expertise to make these changes,\" Cassidy, a physician, wrote on X. \"Vaccines are overwhelmingly safe. Vaccines are effective. Vaccines DO NOT cause autism.\"\n\nExperts fear that if parents refrain from getting their children vaccinated as a result of the discredited idea that childhood vaccines are linked to autism, it risks the re-emergence of diseases like measles.\n\nMultiple studies have found no link between the MMR vaccine and autism. A recent high-quality study from Denmark in 2019 looked at 657,461 children and concluded that the data did not support the MMR vaccine causing or triggering autism.\n\nOn its website, the CDC recommends that two doses of the combined MMR vaccine be given to children, starting with the first dose at 12-15 months and the second between the ages of four and six.\n\nHow often and how many vaccines children in the US receive is based on the times when vaccines will work best with their immune systems and when they are most vulnerable to certain diseases, according to the AAP.\n\nThere is no medical reason to delay or skip recommended immunisations, the AAP says.\n\nTrump's order also calls for the development of alternatives to aluminium in vaccines, and to test if those alternatives are safer and more effective.\n\nBut the AAP says studies have found no significant health risks associated with the tiny amounts of aluminium salts in vaccines.\n\nSmall amounts of aluminium salts - which are found naturally in soil, water and foods - have been used in some vaccines for decades in order to boost the body's immune response.\n\nThat means lesser quantities of the vaccine and fewer doses are needed, according to AAP."
