    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "BASL urges Government to withdraw 22nd Amendment\n\nColombo, August 12 (Daily Mirror) - The Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) has urged the Government to refrain from proceeding with the proposed 22nd Amendment to the Constitution in its present form, warning that the proposed extension of the retirement age of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges would directly affect the independence of the Judiciary.\n\nThe BASL issued the statement today (12), shortly before a scheduled meeting with President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to discuss concerns surrounding the proposed constitutional amendment.\n\nThe BASL said it had grave concerns over the Bill, which was published in the Gazette on August 7, and questioned the Government\u2019s stated justification that the amendment was intended to expedite hearings and improve the efficiency of the judicial system.\n\nAccording to the BASL, the correlation between extending the retirement age of superior court judges and achieving those objectives remained unclear.\n\nThe legal community, including the BASL and the Judicial Service Association, had unanimously resolved to oppose the proposed amendment on the basis that it constituted direct interference with judicial independence, the statement said.\n\nThe BASL said its concerns had also been echoed by religious leaders, civil society groups, and prominent overseas law and judges\u2019 associations.\n\nThe BASL further maintained that, if the Government proceeded with the amendment despite objections from stakeholders, the proposed constitutional change could only be validly enacted with the approval of the People at a referendum.\n\nThe BASL maintained that Article 107 of the Constitution, which deals with the retirement age of judges of the superior courts, is contained under the heading \u201cIndependence of the Judiciary\u201d in Chapter XV.\n\nIt also referred to Articles 3 and 4(c), under which sovereignty is vested in the People and judicial power is recognised as a component of that sovereignty."
