    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "The BASL maintained that Article 107 of the Constitution, which deals with the retirement age of judges of the superior courts, is contained under the heading \u201cIndependence of the Judiciary\u201d in Chapter XV.\n\nIt also referred to Articles 3 and 4(c), under which sovereignty is vested in the People and judicial power is recognised as a component of that sovereignty.\n\nThe BASL said this determination made clear, in its view, that the proposed amendment was an entrenched provision that could not be validly enacted without the approval of the People at a referendum.\n\nThe BASL also raised concerns over the constitutional implications if the Bill were placed on Parliament\u2019s Order Paper.\n\nIt said any citizen would then have the right to challenge the constitutionality of the proposed amendment before the Supreme Court. Such a challenge, the BASL warned, could place Supreme Court judges in an \u201cembarrassing and constitutionally sensitive position\u201d, as they would be required to determine the legality of an amendment that would extend their own tenure.\n\nThe BASL therefore said that, if the Government decided to proceed with the proposal, the Cabinet should directly submit the Bill to the People at a referendum, thereby avoiding a situation in which the Supreme Court would have to determine the constitutionality of the amendment.\n\nThe BASL also criticised the lack of consultation with principal stakeholders, saying that constitutional amendments of this nature in other jurisdictions had generally been preceded by transparent, inclusive, and lengthy consultative processes.\n\nThe BASL said it considered it its duty to safeguard the independence of the Judiciary, which it described as an indispensable cornerstone of the Rule of Law and constitutional governance.\n\nThe statement was signed by BASL President Rajeev Amarasuriya and Secretary Nalin de Silva."
